  "scenarios": [
    {
      "id": "lpic1-echo-path-diagnosis",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-1",
      "topic": "IPv4 Echo and route diagnosis",
      "unit": "linux-echo-diagnosis",
      "title": "Repair a path when nearby replies are misleading",
      "summary": "Compare a nearby reply, a remote failure and a silent control; repair the actual mask and routing faults without losing management.",
      "objective": "Configure ens3 with an unused conventional address in the supplied 10.96.16.0/20 LAN and keep it up. Use 10.96.16.1 on ens3 for the preferred default and the application path to 172.22.40.20, including any more-specific route. Restore matching Echo replies from 10.96.16.1, 10.96.30.20, 172.22.40.20 and the management peer 10.45.0.20. Preserve the complete ens4 configuration and every 10.45.0.0/24 route. 172.22.40.99 is a deliberately silent Echo control; a missing reply from it is not a repair requirement.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 22,
      "steps": [
        "Compare evidence before changing the network",
        "Repair only the broken application path"
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "The initial /24 reaches the nearby 10.96.19.x peer but treats the other parts of the supplied /20 LAN as remote. The /20 mask is 255.255.240.0: its third-octet block spans16–31. Restoring that prefix makes the supplied gateway and farther LAN peer local. The correct default cannot override the stale /32 application route; remove or repair that more-specific entry. Preserve the separate ens4 management path. A correctly reflected Echo reply shows this virtual round trip, not a running application service. The silent control intentionally produces no reply even after the repair; treating every missing reply as proof of a bad local route would lead to unnecessary changes.",
      "hints": [
        "Compare ip -4 address show with ens3’s supplied LAN; a nearby reply does not prove the entire prefix is correct. A /20 uses mask255.255.240.0, with third-octet blocks of16.",
        "Read every entry in ip -4 route show. The longest matching prefix is chosen before metric; a /32 can override a corrected default. Remove gateway routes on an interface before changing its address if required by this bounded command subset.",
        "Use the separate worked example to see address deletion/addition, a default replacement and removal of a stale more-specific route. Choose Check network for all outcomes; preserve ens4 and the management route. The silent control is deliberately ungraded."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-101-102-objectives/",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ping.8.html",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc792.html",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-route.8.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Finite in-process IPv4 Echo and existing virtual interface/main-route model only. ping -c 1 ADDRESS and ping -4 -c 1 ADDRESS accept supplied numeric IPv4 peers only; output explicitly identifies simulated queries and no measured RTT/TTL. Source-owned peer response and return-prefix facts are separate from the existing outward path inspection. No host command/network, real packets, DNS names in ping, other counts/options, IPv6 Echo, ICMP errors, retries, timers, checksums, loss statistics or application-service verification. Conventional /1–/30 workstation addressing only; /31-/32 host configurations excluded. /32 here is a destination route, not a workstation prefix. No automatic addressing, kernel neighbor tables or full Linux behavior. Source check2026-11-12.",
